PUBLIC RESPONSE: A. Effected objects cannot take nominal tense/aspect markers

Guarani nominal TAM markers (-kue for past/post-stative, -rã for future/destinative) attach to nouns to indicate temporal properties independent of the clause predicate. Effected objects (entities brought into existence by the verb, e.g., “build a house”) are semantically nonexistent prior to the event; therefore they are incompatible with independent nominal tense marking and must remain bare. This restriction is documented in descriptive grammars of Paraguayan Guarani and follows directly from the ontological presuppositions of the markers. Options B–E either impose an obligatory marker that the semantics forbid or posit optionality/special paradigms that do not exist in the language.

(The conclusion rests on general linguistic reasoning about effected-object semantics plus standard descriptions of Guarani nominal tense; direct corpus verification would raise .)}

